; host/tiers.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers`.
;
; This file is not a namespace. It is a `.bl` SOURCE that the bootstrap seed
; compiles with `BeamLisp.AOT/compile-host-file` into ONE exact-named beam
; (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers.beam`) whose top-level `defn`s are plain exported
; functions — the exact call surface every caller already names at
; `Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers`, so no caller is renamed. It is the fourth pinned host
; leaf after `CompilerData`, `Emit` and `Link`, and the same rules apply:
; functions only, compiled `load? false` into a scratch ebin, committed into the
; floor, installed by `Bootstrap.install!/1`, and judged by `bl seed --check`.
;
; WHY A PINNED MODULE AND NOT A NAMESPACE. `Tiers` answers BEFORE rt/core exist.
; `bin/bl`'s generated bootstrap asks it for the boot-tier verdict, `AOTCache`
; asks it for the codegen digest and the build-driver key, and the build driver
; asks it throughout — all of that runs on a previous generation's beams,
; before `BeamLisp.init/0` has interned a single var. A module on that path can
; never itself be an AOT-rebuilt `BeamLisp.Ns.*` namespace (the bootstrap circle
; again), so it is a pinned host beam instead.
;
; ── THE PREBOOT CONSTRAINT: NO Rt, NO core ──────────────────────────────────
;
; The pinned beam must RUN with nothing on the code path but Elixir/OTP and
; itself. That is stronger than "does not call the shim": every BL convenience
; that compiles to a call into the runtime is forbidden, because
; `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` is not loaded at preboot and `core`'s vars are not interned.
; So this file is written in the surface the BEAM itself provides:
;
;   * `'("boot" "std" …)` — a quoted list compiles to a LIST LITERAL, free; a
;     `(list …)` call would compile to a core/Rt call. `@tiers` and
;     `@library_tiers` are therefore deterministic plain literals, spelled
;     once each, with no `def`/module attribute and no core dependency.
;
;   * `erlang/==`, `erlang/=/=`, `erlang/is_list`, `erlang/is_tuple`,
;     `erlang/list_to_binary`, `erlang/element` and `erlang/map_get` are BIFs;
;     `=`, `some?`, `nil?`, `str`, `first`, `true?`… would each compile
;     to an `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` call or a core var fetch. (The macros `cond`/`when`/`or`
;     are FINE — they expand at compile time into `if`/`let`, which emit nothing at runtime.)
;
;   * `Enum`, `Path`, `File`, `System`, `:code` and `:persistent_term` are host
;     modules: Elixir/OTP, on the path in any `elixir` VM, and the same modules
;     the Elixir twin called. `Enum` invokes the BL closures below as plain
;     funs, exactly as it invoked the Elixir captures it replaces.
;
; `test/bl/tiers_test.bl` proves this twice: it compiles this source at a PROBE
; atom and diffs it against the live Elixir module, AND it runs the emitted beam
; in a fresh `elixir` child whose ONLY added code path entry is that beam —
; asserting `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` is not loaded before, during, or after the calls.
;
; ── ABI PARITY IS THE CONTRACT ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Every function below reproduces its Elixir counterpart clause for clause: the
; same traversal, the same evaluation order, the same return types, the same
; exception class (a non-binary `ns` is a `FunctionClauseError` through the
; guard, not a coerced `:library`), and the same ARITY DEFAULTS (`root \\ priv_root()`
; becomes an explicit zero-arity clause that delegates to the one-arity clause).
;
; The exports are EXACTLY the recalled public surface and nothing else — the
; pinned emitter exports EVERY top-level `defn` it compiles, so the three
; helpers the recalled module kept private (`tier_namespaces/1`,
; `source_beneath?/2`, `beneath_directory?/2`) are NOT top-level functions here:
; the memoized wildcard is spelled inside `boot_namespaces`/`build_namespaces`,
; and the inode walk is an inline `loop`/`recur` inside `boot_source?` and
; `build_source?`. An extra helper would be an ABI difference a caller could
; trip over; a missing export is a crash. (The walk is duplicated rather than
; shared for exactly that reason; `test/bl/tiers_test.bl` pins the export set.)
;
; ── TWO SPELLINGS, DELIBERATELY ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The member names are the UNDERSCORE spellings (`priv_root`, `tier_of_ns`,
; `boot_source?`), not the kebab ones the rest of `.bl` writes. A member is an
; Erlang atom spelled as written, and every caller — `bin/bl`, `AOTCache`, the
; `priv/build/*.bl` driver, `priv/lib/**/*.bl` — names this host module at
; `Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers` with the underscore names it already had. A kebab
; `priv-root` would export a name nobody calls.
;
; ── ONE DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE: priv_root's FALLBACK ─────────────────────────
;
; The recalled `priv_root/0` resolves its source-tree fallback from `__DIR__` —
; a COMPILE-TIME absolute path baked into the Elixir beam. A `.bl` file has no
; `__DIR__` (the compiler provides no such macro). The fallback here is the
; checkout's `priv/` under the process working directory, which is the same
; rule `priv/build/embed-asset.bl` (`priv-root`) and `priv/std/vm/generation.bl`
; (`seed-dir`) already carry, and the same ANSWER in every real caller: the
; launcher and the preboot bootstrap run with the tree as their cwd, and when
; they do not, `:code.priv_dir/1` answers (the launcher builds the
; `beam_lisp-<vsn>/ebin` + `priv` symlink shape precisely so it does). The
; code-path branch — the one a running app or a release takes — is identical to
; the recalled module's, and the differential test pins it.

(ns host.tiers)

(defn build_kernel_namespaces
  "The build-tier namespaces a program outside `build/` may require: the graph
   kernel. Everything else in `build/` is the DRIVER — `std/`, `lib/` and
   `compat/` sources must not name it in a `:require` (pinned by
   `BeamLisp.BootBuildBarrierTest`)."
  []
  '("source-graph"))

(defn names
  "Tier directory names, in load-path order."
  []
  '("boot" "std" "lib" "compat" "build" "self"))

(defn library_names
  "Tiers whose namespaces a program may require (excludes `self/`)."
  []
  '("boot" "std" "lib" "compat" "build"))

(defn dirs
  "Absolute tier directories under `root`, existing ones only."
  ([] (dirs (priv_root)))
  ([root]
   (Enum/filter
     (Enum/map (library_names) (fn [t] (Path/join root t)))
     (fn [d] (File/dir? d)))))

(defn boot_dir
  "The `boot/` directory: the CODEGEN tier the compiler key hashes."
  ([] (boot_dir (priv_root)))
  ([root] (Path/join root "boot")))

(defn build_dir
  "The `build/` directory: the build DRIVER tier, hashed into its own key.

   Distinct from `boot/` on purpose. A namespace here decides WHAT to build and
   cannot change an emitted byte, so a change to one must not invalidate every
   beam in the tree."
  ([] (build_dir (priv_root)))
  ([root] (Path/join root "build")))

(defn boot_namespaces
  "Namespaces of the boot tier — the file basenames under `priv/boot/`, which
   is also their declared ns (the tier is flat). Memoised in `:persistent_term`
   under `'(:beam_lisp.tiers.namespaces :boot)`: the AOT drift gate asks on
   every load, and the tier's membership is a constant of the checkout.

   `data-readers.bl` executes registrations into core at boot; it declares no
   namespace and therefore cannot have a namespace BEAM/provenance stamp, so it
   is filtered out of the answer (the memo holds the unfiltered list, exactly
   as the recalled `tier_namespaces/1` cached it)."
  []
  (let [key '(:beam_lisp.tiers.namespaces :boot)
        cached (persistent_term/get key nil)]
    (if (erlang/== nil cached)
      (let [names (Enum/map (Path/wildcard (Path/join (boot_dir) "*.bl"))
                            (fn [p] (Path/basename p ".bl")))]
        (persistent_term/put key names)
        (Enum/reject names (fn [n] (erlang/== n "data-readers"))))
      (Enum/reject cached (fn [n] (erlang/== n "data-readers"))))))

(defn build_namespaces
  "Namespaces of the build tier — file basenames under `priv/build/`. Memoised
   under `'(:beam_lisp.tiers.namespaces :build)`."
  []
  (let [key '(:beam_lisp.tiers.namespaces :build)
        cached (persistent_term/get key nil)]
    (if (erlang/== nil cached)
      (let [names (Enum/map (Path/wildcard (Path/join (build_dir) "*.bl"))
                            (fn [p] (Path/basename p ".bl")))]
        (persistent_term/put key names)
        names)
      cached)))

(defn tier_of_ns
  "Which tier a namespace's freshness key comes from.

     * `:boot` — codegen: `BeamLisp.AOTCache.compiler_key/0`
     * `:build` — the driver: `BeamLisp.AOTCache.build_key/0`
     * `:library` — everything else: the interface closure plus `compiler_key/0`

   The guard is the recalled clause head: a non-binary `ns` is a
   `FunctionClauseError`, never a coerced `:library`."
  [ns]
  :when (string? ns)
  (cond
    (Enum/member? (boot_namespaces) ns) :boot
    (Enum/member? (build_namespaces) ns) :build
    :else :library))

(defn boot_source?
  "Whether a source belongs to the boot tier (codegen), including Mix's priv
   symlink.

   `root` is which priv the question is about, and it exists because a build can
   run over a STAGED copy of a priv rather than the running one — a self-build
   stages the payload's sources so that the paths compiled into beams are stable
   from generation to generation. Without an explicit root every staged source
   would read as ordinary, and the driver's own beams would lose the tier key the
   drift gate depends on.

   Identity is INODE-BASED: the walk compares `{major_device, inode}` of each
   ancestor of `path` against the tier directory's own stat, so a path THROUGH a
   symlink (the launcher's `…/beam_lisp-0.1.0/priv -> <tree>/priv`) is recognised
   by what it points at, not by a string prefix. `File.stat/1` follows symlinks,
   which is what makes that work."
  ([path] (boot_source? path (priv_root)))
  ([path root]
   (let [dir (boot_dir root)
         st (File/stat dir)]
     (if (and (erlang/is_tuple st) (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 st)))
       (let [s (erlang/element 2 st)
             md (erlang/map_get :major_device s)
             ino (erlang/map_get :inode s)]
         (loop [p (Path/dirname (Path/expand path))]
           (let [ps (File/stat p)]
             (if (and (erlang/is_tuple ps)
                      (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 ps))
                      (erlang/== md (erlang/map_get :major_device (erlang/element 2 ps)))
                      (erlang/== ino (erlang/map_get :inode (erlang/element 2 ps))))
               true
               (let [parent (Path/dirname p)]
                 (if (erlang/== parent p) false (recur parent)))))))
       false))))

(defn build_source?
  "Whether a source belongs to the build-driver tier, relative to `root`."
  ([path] (build_source? path (priv_root)))
  ([path root]
   (let [dir (build_dir root)
         st (File/stat dir)]
     (if (and (erlang/is_tuple st) (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 st)))
       (let [s (erlang/element 2 st)
             md (erlang/map_get :major_device s)
             ino (erlang/map_get :inode s)]
         (loop [p (Path/dirname (Path/expand path))]
           (let [ps (File/stat p)]
             (if (and (erlang/is_tuple ps)
                      (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 ps))
                      (erlang/== md (erlang/map_get :major_device (erlang/element 2 ps)))
                      (erlang/== ino (erlang/map_get :inode (erlang/element 2 ps))))
               true
               (let [parent (Path/dirname p)]
                 (if (erlang/== parent p) false (recur parent)))))))
       false))))

(defn toolchain_source?
  "Whether a source is a TOOLCHAIN source: codegen (`boot/`) or driver
   (`build/`). These build serially before the ordinary waves — the compiler
   and the program scheduling it must both be sound before anything else runs."
  ([path] (toolchain_source? path (priv_root)))
  ([path root] (or (boot_source? path root) (build_source? path root))))

(defn source_extensions
  "Loadable source suffixes. This preboot-safe inventory is shared by tier
   builds and the loader; it needs neither namespace vars nor other BL hosts."
  []
  '(".bl" ".bl.md" ".bl.org" ".clj" ".cljc"))

(defn sources
  "Every loadable source under the library tiers, sorted. The tiers own the
   supported extensions, including literate sources; builds and packaged source
   inventories must not silently omit namespaces the runtime can load."
  ([] (sources (priv_root)))
  ([root]
   (let [exts (source_extensions)]
     (Enum/sort
       (Enum/uniq
         (Enum/flat_map (dirs root)
           (fn [d]
             (Enum/flat_map exts
               (fn [ext] (Path/wildcard (Path/join d (erlang/list_to_binary (erlang/++ '(42 42 47 42) (erlang/binary_to_list ext))))))))))))))

(defn priv_root
  "beam-lisp's `priv/` directory. Prefers the code path (works in releases),
   falling back to the source tree (escripts, flat deployments, the compile
   task's VM before the app is loaded).

   An escript answers `priv_dir` with a path INSIDE its archive — a path no
   `File`/`Path.wildcard` call can see. Treat a priv dir that is not a real
   directory as absent and fall back to the source tree: otherwise
   `boot_namespaces/0` reads as `[]`, the drift gate asks `build-plan` for a key
   while `build-plan` is the namespace being loaded, the loader's cycle guard
   answers 'already loading', and the escript dies at boot with
   `undefined var: build-plan/key-for`."
  []
  (let [d (code/priv_dir :beam_lisp)]
    (if (and (erlang/is_list d) (File/dir? (erlang/list_to_binary d)))
      (erlang/list_to_binary d)
      (let [tree (Path/join (System/cwd) "priv")]
        (if (File/dir? tree) (Path/expand tree) (Path/expand "priv"))))))
